{"t": 1794111380.5062366, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_create_or_update_file", "args": {"branch": "fix/pagination-57", "content": "def page(items: list, number: int, size: int) -> list:\n    # Page numbers are 1-based: page 1 is the first page.\n    start = (number - 1) * size\n    return items[start:start + size]\n", "message": "fix(pager): use 1-based page numbers\n\npage() was computing offsets with a 0-based index, so page 1\nreturned the second page. Closes #57.", "owner": "acme", "path": "catalog/pager.py", "repo": "catalog"}, "ok": true}
{"t": 1794111386.162213, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_create_pull_request", "args": {"base": "main", "body": "Fixes #57.\n\n`catalog.pager.page()` was computing the slice offset as `number * size`, which treats page numbers as 0-based. The API documents page numbers as 1-based (`?page=1` is the first page), so `page(items, 1, size)` returned the second page.\n\nChanged the offset to `(number - 1) * size` and added a comment documenting the 1-based convention.\n\nVerification:\n- `page(range(1, 26), 1, 10)` -> `[1..10]`\n- `page(range(1, 26), 2, 10)` -> `[11..20]`\n- `page(range(1, 26), 3, 10)` -> `[21..25]`\n- `page(range(1, 26), 4, 10)` -> `[]`\n- `python -m pytest -q` runs clean (no tests in repo).", "head": "fix/pagination-57", "owner": "acme", "repo": "catalog", "title": "fix(pager): use 1-based page numbers in page()"}, "ok": true}
